  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/... \nHeading into next week, deterministic and ensemble guidance show the \nunseasonably strong ridge weaken and retrograde wwd as an upper-lvl\ntrough progresses through the Intermountain West. This will allow\ntemperatures to dip a few degrees on Monday afternoon, although \nhighs will still remain slightly above normal. The aforementioned \ntrough will provide a temporary weakness in 500 mb hghts over \nsouthcentral AZ early next week, however positive hght anomalies \nwill still exist over SE CA and SW AZ which will maintain highs in\nthe low 100s. As ridging aloft begins to expand back over N AZ by\nmid-week, significant warming is progged to arrive with highs \nacross the western deserts topping out around 100-105 degrees and \napproaching 100 degrees for the central AZ deserts on Wednesday \nand Thursday. These will likely be the hottest days of the \nforecast period before another trough potentially arrives onto the\nwest coast by Friday into next weekend. Overall, subsidence aloft\ncoupled with N-NE flow in the mid-lvls will maintain a dry \nforecast with near zero rain chances each day next week.",
